WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:02.020
Hello!

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:07.710
In today's episode of Bug Bounty Reports Explained
I will show how it was possible to send a

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:14.779
malicious GIF image in WhatsApp that could
take control over whole Android smartphone.

00:00:14.779 --> 00:00:18.870
The video is based on an article by a guy
named Awakened.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.500
Link, as always is in the description.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.830
Low-level bug is something new on my channel
and I hope you will enjoy that!

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.260
Let's start with a GIF file.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:36.260
It contains multiple images and when those
frames are being shown one after another,

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.460
they create an animation.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.739
Usually, during such animation, frames have
the same size.

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:48.720
However, this is not enforced by GIF specification.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:55.360
Frames can have different sizes, including
frames of size 0.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:03.020
This is the structure used to store information
about GIFs from native Android library.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.850
Most important for us is the rasterBits buffer.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:11.250
It's where decoded frames are being stored.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:17.780
In a typical GIF where all frames have the
same size, this buffer is just reused.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:24.889
However, when width or height of a new frame
is greater than the previous one, the buffer

00:01:24.889 --> 00:01:26.774
must be reallocated.

00:01:27.523 --> 00:01:32.090
Reallocation is a combination of free and
malloc functions.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:37.110
Malloc is a function used to allocate space.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:42.439
You need to use it when you create a new variable
or an object.

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:48.119
Free is the function used to free the memory
after it's no longer needed so that it can

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:51.770
be assigned to another variable.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:57.850
And coming back to our GIFs, the amount of
space to reallocate is just the size of the

00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:59.759
new frame.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:07.659
So if a GIF would have frames of sizes 4x4,
3x3 and 5x5, then after the first frame, there's

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:13.530
no need for reallocation, but after the second
frame the buffer would be reallocated with

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:16.730
25 bytes of space.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:24.170
But what would happen if a new frame would
have size 5x0?

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:30.900
For the library it was enough if a new width
or new height was greater than the one in

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.810
the previous frame.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:38.070
So with frame 5x0, the reallocation would
happen.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:45.780
But the malloc would try to reallocate 5*0=0
bytes, so it would simply do nothing and the

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.630
reallocation would only free the memory.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:57.950
So if we would have frames of sizes 4x4, 5x0,
5x0, info-&gt;rasterBits would be freed after

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:01.010
the first AND after the second frame.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.850
And this is the double-free vulnerability.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:07.580
But the question is - why is that actually
a problem?

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:15.400
It's the problem because in Android it leads
to 2 subsequent memory allocations returning

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.400
the same address.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:24.510
Size of those two reallocations must be the
same as the size of double-freed buffer.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:32.150
So when the first frame was 16 bytes and it
was double-freed, then the next 2 memory allocations

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:36.620
of size 16 will return the same address.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.905
Now let's see how this worked in WhatsApp.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:49.150
When you opened preview of files from WhatsApp
application, GIFs were parsed twice.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:54.140
Let's create a malicious GIF that will trigger
the double-free vulnerability.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:04:02.700
It contains 4 frames and their sizes are:
168, 0 bytes, 0 bytes and the size of the

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.680
4th frame doesn't really matter.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.340
It's just there so the GIF is valid.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:16.530
Before parsing frames, GifInfo object is allocated
with 168 bytes.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:21.099
This number is not dependent from the size
of the first frame.

00:04:21.099 --> 00:04:27.330
It's just that the GifInfo struct has 21 properties,
8 bytes each.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:33.780
And we chose the size of the first frame to
match this number and not the other way around.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.210
You'll see later why we needed that.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:40.080
Now it's time to parse the frames.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:47.749
First, rasterBits buffer is allocated 168
bytes for the first frame.

00:04:47.749 --> 00:04:53.289
Then, it's freed twice, while parsing frames
2 and 3.

00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:58.319
Parsing frame 4 does not cause any problems.

00:04:58.319 --> 00:05:07.150
So now, after this buffer was double freed,
next 2 allocations of size 168 will return

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:09.000
the same address.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Let's now see what happens during the second
parse.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:19.690
Again, 168 bytes are allocated for a new GifInfo
object.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:27.659
Then, the first frame is being parsed and
again, 168 bytes are allocated for it.

00:05:27.659 --> 00:05:32.639
So this is the moment, when malloc returns
the same address again.

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:39.400
So now both info and info-&gt;rasterBits are
pointing to the same address.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.690
So such GIF causes WhatsApp to crash.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:48.460
But it wasn't our goal, our goal was to execute
arbitrary commands.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:53.409
Let's now take a look how this could be achieved.

00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:59.629
Soon after parsing each frame, info-&gt;rewindFunction
was called.

00:05:59.629 --> 00:06:07.110
Since info object has the same size and address
as our first frame, the program now thinks

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:14.199
that our first frame is the info object so
we have complete control over it.

00:06:14.199 --> 00:06:18.409
And the program remembers where the rewindFunction
was.

00:06:18.409 --> 00:06:24.050
So now, we can prepare our first frame so
that when the program wants to execute that

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:28.690
function, it executes whatever we want.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:29.960
And what do we want?

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:33.639
We want a reverse shell.

00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:38.650
Now stay calm people, because it's time for
some assembly.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:45.589
To reach our goal, we need to set proper values
to PC and X0 registers.

00:06:45.589 --> 00:06:51.580
Registers in assembly are like special variables
that contain specific information.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:57.099
PC (program counter) stores the address of
the next instruction.

00:06:57.099 --> 00:07:00.849
So we will want the address of the system
function there.

00:07:00.849 --> 00:07:06.110
And the get it there, we need to replace the
address of the rewindFunction.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:12.800
And X0 register stores the first function
argument, so we want to put our reverse shell

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.020
command in there.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:22.580
So now, the researcher had to find a function
that will set the X0 register to what he wanted

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:26.289
and then jump to the system function.

00:07:26.289 --> 00:07:29.530
And this is the gadget that he found.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:35.919
I will explain it while at the same time creating
the first frame of the GIF, which will at

00:07:35.919 --> 00:07:39.490
the same time be a GifInfo object.

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:47.389
The idea is that from rewindFunction we jump
to this gadget, it sets a proper X0 register

00:07:47.389 --> 00:07:52.009
and the we go to the system function, reaching
our goal.

00:07:52.009 --> 00:07:59.430
Originally, rewindFunction was at offset 0x80
of the info object.

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:03.880
So this is the place where we put the address
of this gadget.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:13.759
Then, ldr instruction from this gadget loads
the value from register X19 with offset 0x18

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:15.199
to register X8.

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:19.240
X8 will be the address where we jump next.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:23.379
It's what blr instruction at the end does.

00:08:23.379 --> 00:08:26.589
And X19 is our first frame.

00:08:26.589 --> 00:08:32.960
So at offset 0x18 we put the address of the
system function.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:40.390
The second instruction loads the value form
our first frame with offset 0x20 to register

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:44.720
X0 which will our argument.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:50.600
So in our frame, at this offset we put our
command with reverse shell.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.340
And that's pretty much the whole exploit.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:09:00.060
The problem is that we need to get the addresses
of our gadget and the system function.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:03.840
And there are 2 practical attack scenarios
for this.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:10.010
One of them is that a malicious application,
installed on the victim's phone gets those

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:16.350
addresses easily, because they are native
libraries and the addresses only change while

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.350
the phone is rebooted.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:25.440
Then, this application would create a malicious
GIF, save on victim's phone and once the victim

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.570
opened a preview in WhatsApp, the exploit
would get triggered.

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:35.960
The other attack scenario is that the attacker
gets those addresses using information disclosure

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:41.580
vulnerability and then the attacker prepares
the GIF, sends to the victim and once the

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:46.090
victim opens the preview in WhatsApp, in gets
triggered.

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:52.320
You can generate your own malicious GIF using
the repo that's linked in the description

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.720
but of course, the vulnerability has been
patched and latest versions of WhatsApp are

00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:59.820
no longer exploitable.

00:10:00.589 --> 00:10:06.900
The vulnerability affected WhatsApp but it
was actually a bug in native Android library

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:12.020
so I don't think the researcher got any bounty
for it, because WhatsApp security team just

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:15.430
forwarded the report to library maintainers.

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:20.780
Lately, both on YouTube and on Twitter I did
a poll, asking you what you would like to

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:25.850
see more on my channel and low-level bugs
were highly rated, so I hope you like this

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:26.850
video.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:31.870
But if you have any other suggestions, feel
free to comment them down below.

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:35.910
If you have learnt something new today, leave
